1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,720
Poor air quality outside doesn't mean you're safe once you're indoors — the EPA's wildfire smoke guidance actually flips a lot of the usual advice.

2
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:20,000
Three habits make indoor air worse on bad air days: cracking the windows, cooking on the stove, and running the vacuum.

3
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,800
First, skip cracking the windows.

4
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:30,560
Opening them during a smoke event or high pollution alert lets outdoor particles straight into your home.

5
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:38,880
Switch your HVAC fan to recirculate mode instead, and swap in a MERV-13 filter if your system can handle it.

6
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:43,680
Second, hold off on frying, broiling, and candles.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,080
Cooking indoors pushes particle levels far higher than what's happening outside, and it can take over an hour to clear.

8
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:58,320
Cook after the air quality improves, or run your range hood on high the whole time.

9
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:06,720
Third, skip the vacuum until levels drop — it stirs settled dust right back into the air.

10
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:14,080
Run a properly sized HEPA air purifier instead, and pick one that produces little to no ozone.

11
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:23,040
Keeping windows shut, holding off on cooking, and running a real air purifier make bad air days far more manageable.
